<.guide_page
  title="How emisar works"
  dek="emisar separates the decision to run an infrastructure action from the machine that runs it. The control plane handles identity, scope, policy, and approvals. A runner on the target host verifies the exact action again, executes it, redacts the output, and records what happened."
  date="July 2026"
  read_time="12 min read"
>
  <p>
    There are two easy ways to let an AI agent help with production, and neither is very good.
    Keep the agent outside and a person has to copy every log, command, and result between chat and
    a terminal. Give it SSH and the copying disappears, along with the boundary between "show me
    the logs" and "delete the data directory."
  </p>
  <p>
    emisar takes a different route. The agent gets a finite catalog of infrastructure actions, not
    a shell. A central control plane decides whether a request is in scope and permitted. A runner
    on the target host checks the request against its own copy of the action before anything runs.
    The model can ask for work; it cannot invent the program the host will execute.
  </p>
  <p>
    That split is the whole system in miniature: centralized identity and policy, distributed
    enforcement and execution. Let's build it back up from the bottom.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with the thing that runs: an action</h2>
  <p>
    An action is a command contract written before the request arrives. Take <code>linux.systemctl_restart</code>. Its definition names the program, lays out the argument
    vector, declares a <code>unit</code> argument and its validation rules, sets a timeout and
    output limits, assigns a risk level, and defines any extra redaction patterns. The caller
    supplies the unit name. It does not supply <code>systemctl</code>, the verb <code>restart</code>, or a command line for a shell to interpret.
  </p>
  <p>
    These are the execution-relevant fields from the action that ships in the
    <code>linux-core</code>
    pack:
  </p>

  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="linux-core/actions/systemctl_restart.yaml"><span class="text-sky-300">id</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">linux.systemctl_restart</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">kind</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">exec</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">risk</span>: <span class="text-amber-300">high</span>

<span class="text-sky-300">args</span>:
  - <span class="text-sky-300">name</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">unit</span>
    <span class="text-sky-300">type</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">string</span>
    <span class="text-sky-300">required</span>: <span class="text-violet-300">true</span>
    <span class="text-sky-300">validation</span>:
      <span class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-all text-brand-300"><span class="text-sky-300">pattern</span>: "^[A-Za-z0-9@._:][A-Za-z0-9@._:-]&#123;0,127&#125;$"</span>
      <span class="text-sky-300">max_length</span>: <span class="text-amber-300">128</span>

<span class="text-sky-300">execution</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">command</span>:
    <span class="text-sky-300">binary</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">systemctl</span>
    <span class="text-sky-300">argv</span>: [<span class="text-brand-300">"restart"</span>, <span class="text-brand-300">"&#123;&#123; args.unit &#125;&#125;"</span>]
  <span class="text-sky-300">timeout</span>: <span class="text-amber-300">60s</span>

<span class="text-sky-300">output</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">max_stdout_bytes</span>: <span class="text-amber-300">4096</span>
  <span class="text-sky-300">max_stderr_bytes</span>: <span class="text-amber-300">8192</span></.docs_code>

  <p>
    Actions travel in versioned <strong>packs</strong>. A pack contains its manifest, action
    definitions, argument schemas, and any scripts those actions own. The complete pack tree is
    hashed. That hash, not just a friendly version such as <code>1.4.0</code>, identifies the exact
    bytes an operator reviewed and trusted. Change an argument rule, a script, or one character of
    the command and the hash changes with it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most actions execute a binary directly with an argument array. Some jobs genuinely need shell
    features such as a pipe, so a pack can carry a fixed, reviewed <code>/bin/sh -c</code>
    program.
    Even there, the program comes from the pack and every substituted value must pass a bounded
    schema. There is also an explicit arbitrary-shell pack for staging and break-glass work. It is
    critical-risk and denied by default. Calling it a sandbox would be comforting and false.
  </p>

  <h2>The runner is the execution plane</h2>
  <p>
    The runner is a small Go program installed on each host emisar can reach. It opens an outbound
    TLS WebSocket to the control plane. It does not listen on an inbound port, so adding a runner
    does not add a new network service for the internet to find.
  </p>
  <p>
    At startup, the runner loads the packs on disk, validates their manifests and referenced
    scripts, computes their content hashes, and applies the host's local admission rules. Those
    rules can hide or refuse actions by ID or cap the maximum risk that this host will accept. The
    runner then advertises the catalog it can actually enforce. The control plane receives action
    descriptors and hashes, not a new path for uploading programs to the host.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a dispatch arrives, the runner does not assume the cloud already got everything right. It
    looks up the action locally, re-hashes the pack, compares it with the trusted hash in the
    request, applies local admission again, rejects unknown arguments, validates every value, and
    clamps timeouts and output limits to the action's declared envelope. Only then does it render
    the pack-authored argument vector and call <code>os/exec</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The child process receives a minimal environment and the OS permissions of the runner service
    user, unless the action deliberately drops to another configured user. Output is bounded and
    passed through the runner's redaction pipeline before a progress chunk leaves the machine.
    Timeouts and cancellation terminate the process group, not merely the first process in a
    pipeline.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is deliberate double validation. The cloud catches a bad request early. The host checks
    the exact contract again at the last useful moment, against the bytes and policy it actually
    has.
  </p>

  <h2>The control plane decides what may run</h2>
  <p>
    The hosted control plane owns the questions that need a shared view of the organization: who
    is calling, which account they belong to, which runners they can reach, which pack hashes an
    administrator trusts, and what policy applies to the requested action on each target.
  </p>
  <p>
    Policy has three outcomes. An allowed request can continue immediately. A denied request stops
    without creating work for the runner. A request that requires approval creates a pending
    decision bound to that action, pack, arguments, target, and reason. Approval is a branch for
    the actions that need a person, not a toll booth in front of every useful read.
  </p>
  <p>
    The control plane never executes the host command itself. Its job is to authenticate, scope,
    check pack trust, evaluate policy, hold approvals, dispatch eligible work, and keep the
    searchable fleet record. Denials belong in that record too. They are often the clearest signal
    that an agent was confused, overreaching, or following hostile text it found while
    investigating.
  </p>

  <h2>The MCP interface stays small while the catalog grows</h2>
  <p>
    Publishing one MCP tool for every infrastructure action sounds natural until a fleet exposes
    hundreds of them. Every client then has to load hundreds of names, descriptions, target lists,
    and schemas before the model can choose anything. Add a pack and the tool surface changes
    again.
  </p>
  <p>
    emisar exposes twelve fixed MCP tools instead. Discovery moves through <code>list_packs</code>, <code>find_actions</code>, and <code>get_action</code>. Execution goes
    through <code>run_action</code>. Separate tools cover waiting, recent runs, operation recovery,
    and runbooks. Installing another pack adds actions to the searchable catalog; it does not add
    another MCP server or another page of tool definitions to every client.
  </p>
  <p>
    The catalog is not a public wish list. Results are derived from the caller's current runner
    scope, exact operator-trusted pack manifests, and what connected runners actually advertise.
    An action that is outside scope, untrusted, retired, missing from the host, or inconsistent
    with the trusted descriptor is not presented as executable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Local clients use the small <code>emisar-mcp</code>
    bridge to translate stdio MCP into the same
    authenticated HTTP API used by remote clients. The bridge holds the credential and handles
    transport details. It does not grow its own policy engine or reinterpret what the control plane
    decided.
  </p>

  <h2>Packs can turn internal workflows into agent tools</h2>
  <p>
    Packs are not limited to familiar infrastructure commands. A support workflow, internal API,
    or release operation can become an action when it can be named, given typed inputs, and routed
    through a narrow implementation. The agent still gets a catalog of specific capabilities, not
    the underlying admin console.
  </p>
  <p>
    We use that pattern ourselves. A private <code>emisar-admin</code>
    pack lets our LLM agents handle real customer-service work: find and inspect accounts, diagnose
    access, resend invitations, reset MFA, revoke sessions, sync billing, manage complimentary
    plans, and operate account and member lifecycles. The same pack exposes bounded product
    analytics and runtime health checks. An agent can help a customer without receiving a database
    console or a general portal RPC.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pack runs only on dedicated administration runners beside the portal and is never published
    in the public catalog. Every action still declares its arguments, risk, side effects, timeout,
    and output bounds, then crosses one fixed release-RPC boundary to a named operation. A read-only
    action such as <code>emisar.admin.access.diagnose</code>
    can run under a different policy from an MFA reset, an account suspension, or a permanent
    erasure. Creative use does not mean weakening the boundary; it means applying the same boundary
    to more useful work.
  </p>

  <h2>One request, end to end</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose an agent has traced a checkout failure to a wedged service on <code>api-iad-02</code>. The request path looks like this:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      The agent searches the catalog, then fetches the exact action contract. It receives the
      immutable pack reference, argument schema, risk, side effects, and compatible runner
      references.
    </li>
    <li>
      It calls <code>run_action</code> with the action ID, pack reference, explicit runner
      references, structured arguments, and a human-readable reason. It can also state the evidence
      it observed and the result it expects.
    </li>
    <li>
      The control plane resolves every runner reference inside the caller's scope, confirms that
      each connected runner advertises the exact trusted action, validates the portable part of the
      schema, and evaluates policy for every target.
    </li>
    <li>
      Allowed runs get dispatch work. Denied runs are recorded but never delivered. Runs that need
      approval wait without a dispatch job until an authorized person approves the exact request
      and the control plane rechecks the gates.
    </li>
    <li>
      The eligible dispatch travels down the WebSocket the runner opened. The runner repeats its
      local hash, admission, argument, target, and limit checks before it crosses the process
      boundary.
    </li>
    <li>
      The runner executes the action, streams bounded redacted progress, records its local decision
      and outcome, and returns the terminal result. The control plane updates the run and the cloud
      audit trail.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    For the running example, the mutation request is structured data. The pack hash and runner
    reference are exact identifiers returned by discovery; they are shortened below only to keep
    the example readable. The agent does not get to substitute a friendlier name at execution time.
  </p>

  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="run_action input">&#123;
  <span class="text-sky-300">"action_id"</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"linux.systemctl_restart"</span>,
  <span class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-all text-brand-300"><span class="text-sky-300">"pack_ref"</span>: "linux-core@0.3.19/sha256:8d0ba17c…701c70ba"</span>,
  <span class="text-sky-300">"runner_refs"</span>: [
    <span class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-all text-brand-300">"api-iad-02~18a65e2f…6d2fc9"</span>
  ],
  <span class="text-sky-300">"args"</span>: &#123;<span class="text-sky-300">"unit"</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"checkout.service"</span>&#125;,
  <span class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words text-brand-300"><span class="text-sky-300">"reason"</span>: "Restart checkout: health checks show the unit is wedged."</span>,
  <span class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words text-brand-300"><span class="text-sky-300">"evidence"</span>: "Status reported ActiveState=failed."</span>,
  <span class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-words text-brand-300"><span class="text-sky-300">"expected"</span>: "Unit returns to active; checkout health check passes."</span>
&#125;</.docs_code>

  <p>
    What comes back is a bounded run summary, not a terminal session. If the action finishes inside
    the wait window, the agent receives the outcome directly. Empty output streams are omitted; any
    output that is present has already passed through the runner's redaction and size limits. These
    are the fields that matter most in a successful result:
  </p>

  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="selected fields from the completed result">&#123;
  <span class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-all text-brand-300"><span class="text-sky-300">"operation_id"</span>: "op_01K09E3N8Y4J6T2D5Q7R1V9WCF"</span>,
  <span class="text-sky-300">"runs"</span>: [&#123;
    <span class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-all text-brand-300"><span class="text-sky-300">"run_id"</span>: "019f61cf-59b4-71d9-a78c-4ece74d1e163"</span>,
    <span class="whitespace-pre-wrap break-all text-brand-300"><span class="text-sky-300">"runner_ref"</span>: "api-iad-02~18a65e2f…6d2fc9"</span>,
    <span class="text-sky-300">"status"</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"success"</span>,
    <span class="text-sky-300">"exit_code"</span>: <span class="text-amber-300">0</span>,
    <span class="text-sky-300">"duration_ms"</span>: <span class="text-amber-300">380</span>
  &#125;]
&#125;</.docs_code>

  <p>
    The same durable <code>operation_id</code>
    ties retries to the original request. If an HTTP response is lost, the bridge can recover the
    operation instead of casually starting it again. At the other end, the runner's replay store
    refuses to turn a repeated dispatch into a second process or accept different facts under the
    same identity. A nonterminal response says what stopped progress — for example
    <code>pending_approval</code>
    — and returns the exact continuation the agent should call next.
  </p>

  <h2>The host keeps the last word, with one important qualification</h2>
  <p>
    Local admission can always make a runner stricter than the cloud: a production image can omit
    write actions, deny a namespace, or cap the host at low-risk work. The runner also refuses an
    unknown action, a changed pack, an invalid argument, an excessive timeout, or a stale target
    reference even if the control plane sends it.
  </p>
  <p>
    This host configuration vetoes the restart example even if cloud policy allows it:
  </p>

  <.docs_code phx-no-format label="/etc/emisar/config.yaml"><span class="text-sky-300">admission</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">allow</span>:
    - <span class="text-brand-300">"linux.*"</span>
  <span class="text-sky-300">deny</span>:
    - <span class="text-brand-300">"linux.systemctl_restart"</span>
  <span class="text-sky-300">max_risk</span>: <span class="text-amber-300">medium</span></.docs_code>

  <p>
    That does not mean the hosted control plane is absent from the trust model. In the normal mode,
    it is the authority that authenticates the caller, evaluates policy, and sends eligible
    dispatches. If your threat model includes a compromised control plane, signed dispatch adds a
    stronger boundary: the operator's MCP bridge signs the exact action, pack, arguments, targets,
    reason, operation, nonce, and time with a key the cloud does not hold. An enforcing runner
    verifies that claim before execution. The cloud can still delay or withhold the request; it
    cannot forge a new one, alter it, widen its targets, or replay it successfully.
  </p>
  <p>
    The deepest trust anchor is still the host. A pack is executable configuration, and the runner
    can do whatever its service user is allowed to do. Operators decide which pack bytes to trust
    and which OS privileges to grant. A compromised host already controls the machine emisar is
    trying to operate; this system does not pretend to contain it from itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Two records answer different questions</h2>
  <p>
    The cloud audit is the searchable system of record across the fleet. It ties the caller,
    request, policy decision, approval, target, execution status, and returned evidence together.
    Because denied and pending requests are included, it shows intent and enforcement, not only
    commands that completed.
  </p>
  <p>
    A runner that receives a dispatch also appends security events to a local JSONL journal. Each
    line includes the hash of the previous line, so deleting a middle entry, changing one, or
    reordering the retained file breaks verification. The runner records that execution started
    immediately before launching the child and later records the terminal outcome, so a crash can
    leave an unmatched start instead of erasing the fact that work began.
  </p>

  <p>
    The local chain is forensic evidence, not magic paper. A privileged attacker can replace the
    whole file. The cloud audit remains the durable fleet record; environments that need stronger
    retention can export it to storage with their own immutability controls.
  </p>

  <h2>What emisar does not make safe</h2>
  <p>
    emisar is not a VM, a container, or a kernel sandbox. If policy permits a Cassandra restart,
    Cassandra will restart. A well-bounded destructive action still has a real blast radius. The
    system narrows who can request it, the exact shape the request may take, where it may run, and
    whether a person must approve it. It does not make the side effect imaginary.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read-only also describes mutation, not confidentiality. Logs and config output can contain a
    secret no redaction rule recognizes. Redaction happens before output leaves the runner and is a
    useful backstop, but it is pattern-based. Actions should avoid producing secrets in the first
    place, and output from production should still be treated as confidential.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, emisar is not the agent loop. Claude, Cursor, ChatGPT, or another MCP client decides
    what to investigate and what to request next. emisar supplies the governed action surface. The
    model can still form a bad hypothesis; the point is that a bad hypothesis has to pass through a
    declared contract, current scope, policy, and the host's final checks before it becomes a
    process on production.
  </p>

  <h2>Deployment can grow one action at a time</h2>
  <p>
    A useful first deployment is intentionally small: one non-critical host, an unprivileged runner
    user, and a few read actions whose pack bytes you have reviewed. Let low-risk inspection run
    without ceremony. Deny writes until there is a specific operational job worth exposing, then
    add that action with a tight schema and choose whether policy should allow it or hold it for a
    person.
  </p>
  <p>
    From there, expansion is additive. Connect another host. Trust another exact pack. Wrap one of
    your own procedures as an action. The MCP integration stays put while the useful vocabulary
    grows, and every new verb arrives with its limits, policy, and record attached.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the access-pattern argument behind this design, read <.link navigate={
      ~p"/guides/give-ai-agents-safe-production-access"
    }>how to give an AI agent safe production access</.link>.
    For the hostile-input case, read <.link navigate={~p"/guides/prompt-injection-for-ops-teams"}>why your production logs are prompts now</.link>.
  </p>
</.guide_page>
